If a fasting person intentionally breaks the fast in a way that necessitates kaffārah, then kaffārah is:

1. freeing one slave, even if not a believer;  
2. if unable, fasting two consecutive months, with no Eid days or days of tashrīq during them;  
3. if unable, feeding sixty destitute people. Each may be given a filling lunch and supper, or two lunches on two days, or two suppers on two evenings, or a supper and a pre-dawn meal. Alternatively, each destitute person may be given half a sā‘ of wheat, flour, or sawīq, or one sā‘ of dates or barley, or the value of one of these foods. [1]

The excerpts also state that certain intentional acts in Ramadan necessitate both kaffārah and qaḍā’, such as intercourse, eating, and drinking consumable items from which nourishment or medicinal benefit is derived, along with the other listed cases. [2][5]

If you need to apply this to a specific fast or action already done, consult a qualified scholar for your actual situation.

If someone intentionally breaks a fast in a way that necessitates kaffārah, then the kaffārah is:

1. freeing one slave, even if not a believer;
2. if unable, fasting two consecutive months, in which there is no Eid nor the days of tashrīq;
3. if unable, feeding sixty destitute people. Each may be given a filling lunch and supper, or two lunches over two days, or two suppers over two evenings, or a supper and a pre-dawn meal. Alternatively, each destitute person may be given half a sā‘ of wheat, flour, or sawīq, or one sā‘ of dates or barley, or the value of one of these foods. [1]

The excerpts also state that kaffārah with qaḍā’ is required when the fast is nullified intentionally, by one’s own free will, and without compulsion, in the listed cases such as intercourse, eating, and drinking consumable items from which nourishment or medicinal benefit is derived. [2]

For an actual case, a qualified scholar should be consulted, since whether kaffārah applies depends on the exact manner in which the fast was broken. [1][2]
